{
  "in_scope": true,
  "search_question": "How can a startup choose its first customers to sell its product to?",
  "reason": "in_scope"
}

{
  "parts": [
    {
      "topic": "Définir précisément le profil du plus petit public viable (langue, budget, problème, traits de caractère) pour choisir l'avenir de l'entreprise",
      "evidence": [
        "S1:E8",
        "S1:E9",
        "S1:E10"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Identifier et cibler les « partenaires de conception » (design partners) avec qui co-créer le produit avant de chercher une croissance à grande échelle",
      "evidence": [
        "S5:E8",
        "S5:E9"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Utiliser la vente dirigée par le fondateur pour aligner la vision sur la réalité du marché et identifier quels segments acceptent cette vision",
      "evidence": [
        "S3:E5",
        "S3:E9",
        "S3:E18",
        "S3:E19"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Se concentrer exclusivement sur un segment spécifique où l'adéquation produit-marché est forte plutôt que de viser tout le marché dès le début",
      "evidence": [
        "S10:E1",
        "S10:E4",
        "S10:E5"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Choisir entre vendre aux petites entreprises (jeu marketing à haut volume) ou aux grandes entreprises (jeu relationnel) en fonction du problème résolu et du modèle souhaité",
      "evidence": [
        "S7:E15",
        "S7:E16",
        "S7:E17",
        "S7:E20",
        "S7:E21"
      ]
    },
    {
      "topic": "Créer des clients de référence initiaux pour rassurer les nouveaux acheteurs qui hésitent à être les premiers à essayer un produit",
      "evidence": [
        "S2:E1",
        "S2:E2",
        "S2:E3",
        "S4:E11",
        "S4:E12"
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "checks": [
    {
      "part_index": 0,
      "verdict": "approved",
      "issue": ""
    },
    {
      "part_index": 1,
      "verdict": "rejected",
      "issue": "The draft claims design partners 'share the same values and needs as the company' and help 'solve technical problems'. The cited evidence (S5:E8, S5:E9) only states that companies find design partners to 'co-create the product with' and figure out 'who is their product resonating with' and 'who is maybe also the decision maker'. The source does not mention shared values, shared needs, or solving technical problems. This is an invention of details not supported by the excerpt."
    },
    {
      "part_index": 2,
      "verdict": "approved",
      "issue": ""
    },
    {
      "part_index": 3,
      "verdict": "approved",
      "issue": ""
    },
    {
      "part_index": 4,
      "verdict": "rejected",
      "issue": "The draft claims small businesses are 'more sensitive to high-volume marketing' and large enterprises are 'more sensitive to the relational game'. The cited evidence (S7:E15-S7:E21) describes small business as a 'marketing game' that is 'high velocity, high volume, lots of dials' and enterprise as a 'very different game'. It does not state that small businesses are 'sensitive' to marketing or that enterprises are 'sensitive' to relationships; rather, it defines the nature of the sales activity required for each. The draft misrepresents the source's description of the sales model as a statement about customer sensitivity."
    },
    {
      "part_index": 5,
      "verdict": "rejected",
      "issue": "The draft claims reference customers 'share the same values and needs as the company'. The cited evidence (S2:E1-E3, S4:E11-E12) discusses creating reference customers because 'most people don't want to be the first to try things' and are likely to try if 'other people that look like you have accepted it'. The source mentions similarity to the *buyer* ('look like you'), not similarity to the *company* ('share values/needs with the company'). This is an unsupported attribution of motive and criteria."
    }
  ],
  "missing_requests": []
}

